rs, a lowest-points rule, or cross-program economic ranking. Preserve each quote's scope: incomplete or unknown-scope amounts do not establish a per-person fare, all-in trip cost or cheapest-overall choice. Give a choice-specific downside or condition only when it changes that choice's decision; do not invent a local downside from a shared unknown. State common missing schedule, quote-scope, seat, taxes or separate-ticket limits once after the choices. Keep any opening sentence consistent with shown choices, or omit the comparison if unsupported. Do not repeat generic availability advice; display adds it separately. Use one k=b SOURCE-BOUND INLINE CASH SENTENCE with the exact benchmark r: plain source origin-to-destination route, quoted amount and material uncertainty. Do not copy this sentence into k=t prose or a card reason, extend the quote to connecting cities, or invent stop descriptors. A loyalty program is not an operating airline; do not guess a missing carrier or borrow one from another record. Never print internal J/A/C aliases. Output JSON blocks in reading order. For cash context rely on its quote.kind, quote.display and quote.source_unit; fee metadata does not describe the cash fare unit. The display already supplies the canonical availability reminder, so leave generic current-availability warnings out of authored prose.\nWrite for the traveler. Call the supplied material search results, not a packet or an internal validation workflow. Report amounts as source quotes and preserve unknown scope. Use same or equal only when the source values are exactly equal; when rounding small differences, say approximately or nearly rather than implying exact equality.",
